;A gallery of plots. Highlight a line and press P P to draw it.
SIN(x)

;Plot this too: it lands in the same window. T traces, Tab jumps to a root
COS(x)

;A family: one curve per element, each in the next color of the palette
[x, x^2/2, x^3/4, x^4/8]

;Poles are gaps, never vertical strokes. Zoom in on one and it stays a gap
TAN(x)

;Only the real half is drawn: left of 0 there is nothing, and no zero line
SQRT(x)

;A Lissajous figure. Plot asks for the t range first; -pi to pi closes it
[3*SIN(3t), 3*SIN(4t)]

;A rose. Switch the window's polar toggle on first, then this is r = f(theta)
3*COS(3t)

;An implicit equation. Equal scales are on, so the circle is round
x^2 + y^2 = 9

;A region: a boolean combination of inequalities, shaded under the curves
x^2 + y^2 < 16 AND y > x

;Simplify this to a matrix (S), then plot it: 49 points, right-click to join
VECTOR([x, SIN(x)], x, -2pi, 2pi, pi/12)

;A saddle. Plot opens a 3D window: drag to orbit, R to spin, Home to reset
x^2 - y^2

;P N for a window of its own. The default 64 by 64 grid does not alias this
SIN(x*y)

;P P puts this in the same box as the one above; the two are the same height
SIN(x) + COS(y)

;P N. A spike at the origin, clipped to the 1st-99th percentile - it says so
1/(x^2 + y^2)

;P N, then type the domain down to -2 and 2: the mesh stops at the circle
SQRT(1 - x^2 - y^2)
